course: dictionary
lesson: 4338
title: Guarantee: a promise you can count on
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A guarantee is a promise that something will happen or will work. As a verb, to guarantee means to promise it surely. «No share is guaranteed to go up; even big famous companies can fall.» Here guaranteed means promised for sure, and the sentence tells you that nobody can promise it.
explain: Shops use guarantees too. A toy with a 2-year guarantee means the shop promises to fix or replace it if it breaks within two years. Look at the words around the word: «flexible hours» with «no guaranteed pay» is a clue that the pay is not promised.
explain: Check the clues in the sentences. «There is no guarantee» means nothing can be promised for certain. «A bonus is extra money on top of normal pay... it is not guaranteed.» A bonus might come, but nobody has promised it. Use the swap test: put promised for sure in place of guarantee and see if the sentence still makes sense.
explain: Maths uses guarantee too. Imagine a drawer with two colours of socks, and you want to be sure of getting a matching pair in the dark. Think of the worst luck first: you could pick one sock of each colour. Then add one more, and you are guaranteed a pair.
fact: A guarantee is a promise that something will happen or will work.
fact: To guarantee means to promise it surely.
fact: A shop guarantee is a written promise to fix or replace something if it breaks within a certain time.
fact: «Nothing can guarantee good weather», so there is no guarantee that the picnic will be dry.
fact: Be careful: «Double your money, guaranteed» is a well-known scam, so a promise is not always true.
exercise: «No share is guaranteed to go up.» What does guaranteed mean here? | Sold at a shop | Easy to count | *Promised for sure | Shown on a map
exercise: A toy has a 2-year guarantee. What does the shop promise? | To give it away after two years | To make it bigger every year | *To fix or replace it if it breaks within two years | To sell it cheaper each month
exercise: «There is no guarantee.» What does this mean? | *Nothing can be promised for certain | Someone is hiding the answer | Everything is promised for certain | The price has gone up
test: What does guarantee mean in «The phone has a one-year guarantee»? | *A promise to fix or replace it if it breaks | A loud sound | A way of counting coins | A kind of phone case
test: To guarantee a matching pair from a drawer with two colours of socks, how many socks must you take? | *3 | 4 | 2 | 1
exam: Which one is a scam rather than a fair promise? | The shop gives a one-year guarantee on the kettle | *«Double your money, guaranteed» | Nobody can guarantee good weather | A guarantee to fix the broken chair within a month
